00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
 
be careful this christmas  rochford district community safety  partnership csp  is encouraging residents to help reduce  burglaries by leaving a light on when no  one is at home during the dark winter  evenings a home without lights on and  curtains left open could be an  invitation to the opportunistic burglar  officers warn it advertises that no one  is at home  rochefort district commander and chair  of the csp chief inspector glenn westley  said our message to residents at this  time of the year is simple get switched  on and leave a light on an empty house  is an attractive target for thieves if  you're going to be out all day at night  or going on holiday set timers for one  or more lights to come on when it gets  dark  automatic time switches are not  particularly expensive and are available  for most diy retailers  there are a number of steps you can take  to keep your home safe during the winter  months  check all doors and windows are shut  and locked before leaving your home is a  really useful routine to establish  some 3 out of 10 burglaries are  committed using open doors or windows  security lights which sense motion are  particularly valuable at front doors as  well as over paths at the side of the  property  a padlock on all side gates add to an  opportunist burglars annoyance  make sure any makeshift ladders such as  wheelie bins are not stored at the front  of your property  make sure if you have a pvc door that  the handle is fully lifted up and the  door locked with the key  drink driving the facts  beware of the morning after if you've if  you're out celebrating in the run-up to  christmas  the body takes an hour to process one  unit of alcohol so if you've had a heavy  night drinking you could still be over  the limit the next morning and in no fit  state to drive to work or tackle the  school run  to recover fully from the effects of a  heavy night of drinking you should avoid  alcohol for a further 48 hours  legally the alcohol limit for drinking  and driving is 80 milligrams of alcohol  per 100 milliliters of blood  that means drinking two pints of normal  strength beer or one large glass of wine  could put you over the limit  take a chance and get behind the wheel  and you risk at least a year's ban a  fine of up to five thousand pounds even  six months in prison  either way you will end up with a  criminal record and that could cost you  your job it will certainly bump up your  insurance premium  more seriously you could kill or injure  someone face up to 14 years in prison  and have to live with it on your  conscience for the rest of your life  myths  a cup of strong coffee will sober me up  caffeine is a stimulant so although it  might make you more alert it won't make  you sober i'll be fine to drive after a  cold shower  a cold shower might be refreshing but it  won't remove alcohol from your system  if i just drink beer and avoid the hard  stuff like spirits i'll be okay  alcohol is alcohol regardless of how you  drink it  keep tag tags on your bike  a bike isn't just for christmas if you  take appropriate steps to safeguard it  from theft  with this in mind rayleigh and rochford  neighborhood watch are planning a  special security tagging session early  in the new year  it will be held at raley library  saturday the 7th of january 2012 between  10 am and 12 noon  the service is free and the tags have  been provided by the rochefort district  community safety partnership  chair of the nhw mike hooper said this  is an ideal opportunity for anyone  buying or expecting to receive a bike  this christmas to come along and have it  fitted with a tag  the individually coded electronic tags  are implanted in the bike's frame and  each owner will be given a special cycle  passport with the code which is also  registered on a national database  this allows police to identify any  recovered bikes and return them to their  owners  stickers can also be attached to the  bike identifying them as having been  tagged as an additional deterrent to  thieves  neighborhood watch with the support of  the csb hold regular bike security  sessions across the district and this  year so far have fitted almost 250  devices  and now for details of our poster design  competition  nobody deserves to be picked on abused  or even assaulted simply because they're  different  but in today's diverse society people  are being victimized because of their  disability ethnic background religion  gender or sexual orientation  it may simply be the use of an  inappropriate and insensitive word or  phrase  at the other extreme it might be far  more serious this is known as hate crime  hate crime takes many forms verbal  insults abusive gestures bullying  offensive graffiti the sending of  malicious letters and text messages  abusive phone calls physical assaults  and even arson  the rochford district hate crime panel  has been set up to promote awareness of  this danger and works with many  different organizations to change  people's attitude to others  the panel has launched a poster design  competition aimed at reaching victims of  hate crime  depending on your age we need your help  to create an a4 poster or a logo that  encourages anyone subjected to hate  abuse to come forward and report it  whether to the police the council  school or bodies such as victim support  now to enter you must be at least 11  years of age and no older than 18 years  of age and there are two age categories  11 to 14 year olds design a logo that  the panel can use on leaflets posters  and displays to encourage victims to get  help  and 15 to 18 years old design an a4  poster for display in public places and  buildings around the district  so people know where and how to report  hate crime  prizes will be awarded to the winning  entries in both age groups  first prize a 50-pound amazon voucher  second prize 25 pounds amazon voucher  and third prize at 10 pounds amazon  voucher  entries must be submitted by monday the  9th of january 2012 at 12 noon  your entry must include your name age  home address telephone contact number  and email address if you have one  you can enter via email to paula.chapman  at rochford.gov dot uk  by post to community safety team  rochford district council council  offices  south street rochford ss4 1bw  the winners will be decided by members  of the hate crime panel who will judge  all the posters and logo designs  received by the closing date  the judge's decision will be final and  both the rochford district hate crime  panel and rochford district council  reserve the right to reproduce all  winning entries owning the copyright as  a condition of entry  entries will not be returned and  therefore please take a copy before you  submit your entry if you wish  dispersal order update  reported incidents of antisocial  behavior in hockley have dropped by  almost a half following the introduction  of a dispersal order covering large  areas of the town the order came into  effect on monday july the 11th to help  curb anti-social behavior and nuisance  and is a joint initiative involving the  police rochford district council and  rochford community safety partnership  the order allows police to disperse  groups of two or more people gathered  within the designated areas which  include maryland's nature reserve  plumber mount and the grounds of  betswood community centre  anyone refusing to leave when asked by  officers or who return within 24 hours  commits an offence and can be arrested  sergeant steve jones from the rochford  west neighborhood policing team said the  order is an excellent means of dealing  with potential incidents before they  escalate and has proven extremely  effective  rochford district council's portfolio  holder for community safety and vice  chairman of the community safety  partnership councillor joe mcpherson  added  this is very encouraging news and the  success of the dispersal order  underlines the power of partnership  working in helping to maintain law and  order in our community  dispersal order figures  in the three months to september police  dealt with 64 reports of antisocial  behaviour in hockley 55 fewer than the  same period in 2010  officers have used their dispersal  powers on 76 separate occasions over the  three months  partnership visits rayleigh  rayleigh residents had the opportunity  to learn more about the services offered  by organizations working in their area  at an information day held at the  women's institute hall bellingham lane  in september  the event was one of a number of days  organized by the castle point and  rochefort community development  partnership across the district  throughout the year  the event brought together a number of  local organizations including the  community safety partnership represented  by tracy schneider and paula chapman  look out for more information day events  in rochford district matters next year  rochford district matters is edited by  patrick john and produced by rochford  district council who can be found at  3-19 south street rochford ss41bw  their telephone number is 01702-546366  and their website is at www.rochford.gov  dot uk  the paper is printed by shaman and  company limited  and that brings us to the end of this  edition which was recorded by volunteers  from the rayleigh rochford and castle  point newspapers for the blind at their  studio in rochford

